KG42.register("rules-c2cba7ce0ed5bdf04406091d",{"id":"05-double_auction::v141-c-R008","paper":"05-double_auction","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R008","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Prosumer participation","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"Prosumers can supply their excess solar energy for trading among neighbors within the microgrid. Scope: Reported microgrid scenario with 70 consumers and 30 prosumers, illustrated in Fig. 4. Condition: Excess solar production between ~07:30 and 16:00 in this scenario.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function participation.prosumer under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["participation.prosumer"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Reported microgrid scenario with 70 consumers and 30 prosumers, illustrated in Fig. 4.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E24"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R008-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Prosumers can supply their excess solar energy for trading among neighbors within the microgrid. Scope: Reported microgrid scenario with 70 consumers and 30 prosumers, illustrated in Fig. 4. Condition: Excess solar production between ~07:30 and 16:00 in this scenario.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E24"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-22913ad574-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"5.1","anchor_text":"5. Results and observations 5. Results are discussed below based on PV penetration levels of the mic","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"Fig. 4 represents the 24-hour load and PV output profiles for a microgrid of 70 consumers and 30 prosumers. Note that the load profile for the 70 consumers is stacked on top of the aggregate load profile for the 30 prosumers. The summation of these two profiles indicate total load of the microgrid. Due to a 30% prosumer to consumer ratio, total prosumer load is lower than the total consumer load. As shown, the peak load of this microgrid is ~500 kW between the hours of 17:00 and 20:00. The total PV capacity in this scenario is 263.9 kW; peak PV output is ~200 kW from 11:00 to 14:00. There is excess solar energy production from the prosumers between ~07:30 to 16:00 that can be traded among neighbors within the microgrid."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"No design-selection or operational decision variable is defined for this participation rule by the source. The only attached passage (v141-b-g02-E24, unreviewed) is a descriptive case-study statement about who participates and when surplus exists; it contains no variable, no range, no choice and no unit-bearing decision quantity tied to participation.prosumer. The scenario counts (30 prosumers, 70 consumers) and profile figures are illustration descriptors, not decision variables.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed parameter with a source-defined symbol is defined for this participation rule. The passage v141-b-g02-E24 supplies numeric case-study descriptors (70 consumers, 30 prosumers, 30% prosumer ratio, 263.9 kW total PV, ~200 kW peak PV, ~500 kW peak load, window ~07:30-16:00) but assigns them no symbol and no model role, so transcribing them as theta would require inventing symbols and meanings; they remain scope/context text inside C001. Hence theta is empty for this rule."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E24","quote":"There is excess solar energy production from the prosumers between ~07:30 to 16:00 that can be traded among neighbors within the microgrid."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"f436c5c8839fb01c5db0a53376a1995301a77ae786668c8a2002ade1ba498a10","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen rule 'Prosumer participation' (V={participation.prosumer}, E=[]) is a procedural participation statement: in the reported microgrid, prosumers supply their excess solar generation for trading among neighbors. The only same-mechanism passage attached to this rule (v141-b-g02-E24, which is UNREVIEWED B evidence and has not passed independent source audit) is a case-study description of the Fig. 4 scenario (70 consumers, 30 prosumers, 263.9 kW total PV, ~200 kW peak PV 11:00-14:00, ~500 kW peak load 17:00-20:00, surplus solar from prosumers ~07:30-16:00 that can be traded among neighbors). It states no equation, no decision variable, no optimization, and defines no symbol tied to the participation.prosumer node. The source-provided quantities (30/70 household counts, kW capacities, clock window) are scenario descriptors of the illustration, not fixed parameters of a participation relation, and no symbol is supplied for them, so they cannot be transcribed as theta without inventing symbols. Accordingly the rule is treated as a non-mathematical (admission/participation) rule that is faithful to the source as a qualitative condition, and the existing constraint C001 is retained unchanged as not_formalized. This is not a claim that the source lacks mathematics elsewhere (mechanism v141-a-M01 does define formulas in other rules); it is a source-scope statement about this participation fact.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R008-C001","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C001 is the core procedural statement of this rule and its sole member is the frozen node participation.prosumer (V of v141-c-R008). The same-mechanism (v141-a-M01) evidence v141-b-g02-E24 is the source of the statement: it describes the 70-consumer/30-prosumer microgrid scenario and the prosumer surplus solar window ~07:30-16:00 that 'can be traded among neighbors within the microgrid'. E of this rule is empty (the frozen graph states no edge incident to participation.prosumer), so no edge_ids are attached; no edge is invented."}],"revision_requests":["If a formalized prosumer-participation condition is desired (e.g., prosumer i may participate only when its excess generation exceeds a threshold over the ~07:30-16:00 window), the frozen graph cannot represent it: V contains only participation.prosumer and E is empty, with no node for prosumer generation/excess quantity or for the local energy product that would be supplied. A C.1 revision adding the corresponding node(s) (and the participation/asymmetric-scope edges) plus same-mechanism evidence with exact symbol definitions is required before any expression can be formalized.","The only supporting passage for this rule (v141-b-g02-E24) is UNREVIEWED B evidence (source_status: unverified; no independent source audit was performed in this experiment). Any downstream use of the scenario numbers (70 consumers, 30 prosumers, 263.9 kW PV capacity, ~200 kW peak PV, ~500 kW peak load, ~07:30-16:00 surplus window) should await B re-extraction/audit confirming the exact source text and the correct reading of the '30% prosumer to consumer ratio' (30/70 vs 30/100)."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
